@@ -0,0 +1,5 @@
+## A field, else a child
+
+`get(key)` looks in three places, in order: an own property of the item, then its `data`, then a member with that id in any List it owns. The last one is what lets a saved line like `{"milk":{"set":{…}}}` find `milk` on replay.
+
+**Watch out:** a getter declared on a class (not an own property, not in `data`) is not found by `get`; read it as a property. Task overrides `get` for its `tasks` for this reason.
diff --git a/public/framework/core/Item/methods/set.md b/public/framework/core/Item/methods/set.md
new file mode 100644
index 000000000..18cfaf4a1
--- /dev/null
+++ b/public/framework/core/Item/methods/set.md
@@ -0,0 +1,11 @@
+## One delta, key by key
+
+`set({ title: "B", count: 2 })` applies each key in order, and each key does one of three things:
+
+- **A method's name** calls it: `set({ remove: [] })` is `remove()`. This is how a saved line replays a verb.
+- **A child's id or a list's name** walks into it: `set({ milk: { set: { count: 2 } } })` changes `milk`.
+- **Anything else** is a field: it goes into `data`, and only if the value changed it emits `change` and `delta`.
+
+`set("title", "B")` is the same as `set({ title: "B" })`.
+
+**Watch out:** an Item or a List passed as a field value is stored as a plain snapshot and never saved again. Give the class a declared list instead ([delta-flow.md](/framework/core/Session/tasks/data-shapes/seats/delta-flow/delta-flow.md), "Would set of a List or an Item wire up persistence?").
diff --git a/public/framework/core/Item/methods/toJSON.md b/public/framework/core/Item/methods/toJSON.md
new file mode 100644
index 000000000..aaa92e30d
--- /dev/null
+++ b/public/framework/core/Item/methods/toJSON.md
@@ -0,0 +1,5 @@
+## The saved shape
+
+`{ "type": "Box", "id": "shop", "data": { … }, "items": [ … ] }`: the type name from `register`, the id, every field under `data`, and each List the item owns as an array under its own name.
+
+`parent`, `store` and `views` are never written, so a saved item cannot point back up. `Item.hydrate` reads this shape back and restores each `parent` by adding the members to their lists.
